A||||10||drednot||can't get into reverse or first.||||11||
Z||000000||drednot||05-05-2001||10:21 AM||||I have an 1985 Fiero.  recently it has been harder and harder to get into reverse and first gear.  It has gotten to the point now that it is very hard to shift gears, almost impossible to get into first and not possible to get it into reverse.  All the gears grind like crazy.  I can get it into reverse when the car is off, but when I start it up in reverse (clutch in)  it drifts backwards sort of like an automatic transmission.  what could cause this??  I checked with my mechanic, he says I am not getting full clutch dis-engagement.  He blead out the system, no luck.  I guess the next step is a transmission re-build.  any thoughts / comments?||||reg||11
Z||000001||Wolfhound||05-05-2001||10:50 AM||||Sounds like a clutch problem, not a transmission problem. <BR>Your gears will grind if your clutch isn't releasing!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||05-05-2001||11:05 AM||||Sounds like a clutch problem, not a transmission problem.  For some reason the clutch isn't completely disengaging.  Possibly you have the bent pedal syndrome.  Check and see if your clutch pedal is aluminum--they are prone to bending and not allowing the clutch to completely disengage<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000003||drednot||05-05-2001||11:51 AM||||The pedal is aluminum.  The problem did not start all at once, it just got worse and worse.  How could GM make a car with a design flaw like that?  Where do I get a new Steel pedal?  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||05-05-2001||12:05 PM||||From GM, the clutch petal arm was changed to steel mid 87. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||LT1 Chop Top||05-05-2001||12:46 PM||||I recently had a the same problem with my 88GT.  I turned out to only be a linkage problem and was very easy to fix.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Gumball||05-05-2001||10:46 PM||||i had the same problem once, I temporarly fixed it by taking out all of the carpet on the driver side.(needed new carpet anyways) Then put in the new carpet when it came in with the steel clutch pedal.||||reg||12
Z||000007||Archie||05-05-2001||11:11 PM||||If you don't want to spend big bucks on a clutch job, spend a little on a new pedal now.<P>Read this <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/arch7.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/arch7.htm</A> <P>Then this <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/arch6.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/arch6.htm</A> <P>Bad pedal<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-593s.jpg"> <P>Good pedal<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-594s.jpg"> <BR>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000008||Chris Thomas||05-07-2001||02:17 AM||||I have a 85 SE 2m6 which had the same exact symptoms.  I followed the steel clutch pedal swap as suggested by archie and that fixed the problem.  Follow the directions posted by archie and I'll bet it fixes your problem.<P>Chris||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrgone||05-07-2001||02:25 AM||||what kind of pedal would my 86 2m4 have?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Jake2m4||05-08-2001||02:24 PM||||I also have an '85 2m4.  I was having the same problems, and steel clutch pedal solved the problem.  I got mine from the Pontiac dealership for like $33.  But now 50 miles later the clutch itself failed.  it is no longer a hydrolic, pedal, or shift linkage problem.  The clutch just stopped engaging completely. <P>However back to the topic, I think a pedal woudl solve the problem.<P>------------------<BR>'85 Fiero Sport Coupe<BR>'95 Pontiac Grand AM SE||||reg||1
